Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for turning that blank “page-background” above the horizon into a proper sky (with optional haze/fog) on a MapLibre GL JS map that already has 3-D terrain.

---

## 1. Make sure you are on a recent version of MapLibre GL JS  

The sky-layer and the `fog` property were added in **v2.4.0** (released 2022).  
If you are still on an older bundle, upgrade:

```html
<!-- CDN – latest stable at the time of writing -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
```

Or, if you use npm:

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl@^2.4
```

---

## 2. Add a terrain source (you already have this)  

```js
map.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

> **Tip:** The `exaggeration` value is optional – tweak it until the hills look right.

---

## 3. Insert a **sky layer**  

A sky layer is a *style layer* of type `"sky"`. It can render either a simple gradient or a full-blown atmospheric model. The atmospheric model gives you a realistic horizon, a sun, and a subtle haze that blends into the terrain.

```js
// Add the sky *after* the terrain source is defined, but before any
// symbol or label layers that you want to appear *in front* of the sky.
map.addLayer({
  id: 'sky',
  type: 'sky',
  paint: {
    // Choose the atmospheric model (most realistic)
    'sky-type': 'atmosphere',

    // Base colour of the sky (you can leave this out – the atmosphere
    // will compute a colour based on sun position)
    'sky-color': '#88ccee',

    // How quickly the colour fades into the horizon (0 = hard line,
    // 1 = very soft). 0.5 is a good starting point.
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

    // Sun position – azimuth (0-360) and altitude (0-90) in degrees.
    // 0, 45 means the sun is due north, 45° up.
    // You can animate this later if you want a moving sun.
    'sky-atmosphere-sun': [0, 45],

    // Sun intensity – higher = brighter, more contrast.
    // Typical values: 5-20.
    'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 15
  }
});
```

### What each paint property does

| Property | Meaning | Typical range |
|----------|---------|---------------|
| `sky-type` | `"gradient"` (simple two-tone) or `"atmosphere"` (realistic) | `"atmosphere"` |
| `sky-color` | Base colour of the sky (only used for gradient or as a fallback) | any CSS colour |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | Blend factor between sky colour and horizon colour | `0 – 1` |
| `sky-atmosphere-sun` | `[azimuth, altitude]` in **degrees** | azimuth `0-360`, altitude `0-90` |
| `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity` | Brightness of the sun | `5-30` (higher = sharper shadows) |

> **Result:** The sky now follows the curvature of the globe, the horizon line is no longer a hard cut, and you get a faint atmospheric glow that fades into the terrain.

---

## 4. (Optional) Add **fog** for extra haze near the ground  

Fog is a *style-level* property (not a layer). It works together with the sky and terrain to give a smooth transition from the ground to the horizon.

```js
map.setFog({
  // The colour of the fog at the farthest distance.
  // Usually you want it to match the sky colour.
  color: 'rgba(255,255,255,0.8)', // light-gray / near-white

  // How quickly the fog fades in. 0 = no fog, 1 = fog starts right at the camera.
  // A value around 0.2-0.4 looks natural for a low-altitude view.
  "horizon-blend": 0.3,

  // Distance (in metres) where the fog starts and where it becomes fully opaque.
  // These numbers are *relative* to the map’s zoom level.
  // Example: start at 0.5 km, become solid at 10 km.
  range: [0.5, 10],

  // Optional: a colour that matches the sky at the horizon.
  // If omitted, MapLibre will blend from `color` to the sky automatically.
  // "high-color": "#88ccee"
});
```

### How fog works

* **`range[0]`** – distance from the camera where the fog begins.
* **`range[1]`** – distance where the fog is fully opaque.
* **`horizon-blend`** – controls how sharply the fog meets the horizon line.
* **`color`** – the colour of the fog at the far end; usually a light version of the sky colour.

You can experiment with the numbers until the ground-to-sky transition feels right for your data.

---

## 5. Full Minimal Example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base raster tiles (you can swap this for any style you like)
        'osm': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        },
        // DEM for terrain
        'terrain': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 14
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }
        // NOTE: we will add the sky layer later via `addLayer`
      ],
      // Optional: set a background colour that will be visible under the sky
      // (e.g., when you zoom out far enough that the globe is not visible)
      background: { type: 'background', paint: { 'background-color': '#e0f7ff' } }
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5], // Berlin
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true // required for terrain & sky to look smooth
  });

  // Wait for the style to load before adding terrain / sky
  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Terrain
    map.addSource('terrain', map.getSource('terrain')); // source already defined in style
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // 2️⃣ Sky layer (must be added *after* terrain source)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'sky',
      type: 'sky',
      paint: {
        'sky-type': 'atmosphere',
        'sky-color': '#88ccee',
        'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
        'sky-atmosphere-sun': [0, 45],
        'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 15
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ Fog (optional but recommended for haze)
    map.setFog({
      color: 'rgba(255,255,255,0.8)',
      "horizon-blend": 0.3,
      range: [0.5, 10]
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What you’ll see**

* The terrain rises and falls in 3-D.
* The horizon is now a smooth curve that blends into a blue-white sky.
* A faint sun disc (you can move it by changing `sky-atmosphere-sun`).
* Light fog that makes distant hills fade into the sky, giving that “haze where the ground meets it” you asked for.

---

## 6. Tweaking Tips & Common Gotchas

| Issue | Fix / Tip |
|-------|-----------|
| **Sky still looks flat** | Make sure the map’s `antialias: true` flag is set (it is required for the sky to render correctly). |
| **Fog looks too strong / too weak** | Adjust `range` (shorter range = thicker fog) and `horizon-blend`. |
| **Sun disappears at high pitch** | The atmospheric model automatically hides the sun when it’s below the horizon. Change the altitude in `sky-atmosphere-sun` to keep it visible. |
| **Background colour shows through** | The sky layer covers the whole viewport, but if you zoom out so the globe is tiny you’ll see the style’s `background` colour. Set it to a sky-like colour (`#e0f7ff`) or make it transparent (`'rgba(0,0,0,0)'`). |
| **Performance drop on low-end devices** | Reduce `terrain.exaggeration` and/or switch `sky-type` to `"gradient"` (much cheaper). |
| **Want a night-time sky** | Use `"sky-type": "gradient"` with dark colours, or set `sky-atmosphere-sun` altitude to a negative value (sun below horizon) and lower `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity`. |
| **Animating the sun (day-night cycle)** | In a `requestAnimationFrame` loop, compute azimuth/altitude from a Date object and call `map.setPaintProperty('sky', 'sky-atmosphere-sun', [az, alt])`. |

---

## 7. Going Further – Custom Sky Textures (if you need something beyond the built-in atmosphere)

MapLibre GL JS does **not** yet support custom sky textures (e.g., a star field) out-of-the-box. If you need that:

1. **Render a skybox** with a separate WebGL canvas behind the MapLibre canvas, or
2. **Use a raster layer** that covers the whole world (e.g., a global equirectangular image) and set its `type: 'background'` with a very low `z-index` (but you lose the curvature effect).

For most applications the built-in atmospheric sky + fog is sufficient and far more performant.

