Below is a complete “how-to” for turning the empty page-background that you see above the horizon into a real sky (with optional atmospheric haze) when you are using MapLibre GL JS + 3-D terrain.

---

## 1. What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.4** | The `sky` layer type and the `fog` property were added in v2.4. If you are on an older version you will only see the plain background. |
| **A DEM source** (e.g. MapLibre Terrain-RGB) | Gives you the 3-D terrain. |
| **A style that contains a `sky` layer** (or you add one yourself) | The sky is drawn as a separate layer that sits on top of the background and below all other layers. |
| **Optional: `map.setFog()`** | Adds a smooth haze/fog that blends the ground into the sky. |

If you already have a map that shows terrain but the area above the horizon is just the page’s background colour, you are missing the `sky` layer (and probably fog).

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain with sky & fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // a very simple base style – you can replace this with any style you like
      sources: {
        // ----  a raster basemap (optional) ----
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        },
        // ----  DEM for terrain  ----
        "terrain": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          url: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 12
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // basemap
        {
          id: "osm",
          type: "raster",
          source: "osm"
        },

        // 2️⃣  Add the sky layer (must be *after* the background but *before* any overlay layers)
        {
          id: "sky",
          type: "sky",
          paint: {
            // Choose one of the three sky types:
            //   "gradient" – simple two-color gradient
            //   "atmosphere" – realistic atmosphere with sun, horizon, etc.
            //   "solid" – single colour (rarely useful)
            "sky-type": "atmosphere",

            // Sun position – azimuth (0-360) and altitude (0-90) in degrees.
            // 0° azimuth = north, 90° = east, 180° = south, 270° = west.
            // Altitude 0° = on the horizon, 90° = zenith.
            "sky-atmosphere-sun": [180, 45],

            // Sun intensity (default 15). Larger = brighter, smaller = dimmer.
            "sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity": 15,

            // Optional: a subtle colour tint for the whole sky.
            // "sky-atmosphere-color": "#88ccee",
            // "sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend": 0.2   // how quickly the colour fades to the horizon
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5], // Berlin
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,            // you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true       // important for smooth terrain rendering
  });

  // 3️⃣  Enable the terrain
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // 4️⃣  Add fog (haze) – optional but highly recommended
    //    Fog works with the built-in sky layer and gives a nice blend
    //    between ground and sky.
    map.setFog({
      // The colour of the fog at the horizon (usually matches the sky colour)
      "color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.8)",

      // How far from the camera the fog starts and ends (in metres)
      // Adjust these numbers to taste – larger range = more subtle fog.
      "range": [0.5, 12],

      // Optional: a colour that the fog fades to at the far end.
      // If omitted it will fade to the sky colour.
      // "high-color": "white"
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | We give the map a tiny inline style that contains a raster basemap (`osm`) and a DEM source (`terrain`). You can replace the basemap with any vector style you like – the sky works the same way. |
| **2️⃣ Add a `sky` layer** | The `type: "sky"` layer is the only thing that draws a sky. The most realistic option is `"sky-type": "atmosphere"`. You can also use `"gradient"` if you just want a simple two-colour fade. |
| **3️⃣ Enable terrain** | `map.setTerrain()` tells MapLibre to use the DEM source for 3-D relief. The `exaggeration` factor is optional. |
| **4️⃣ Add fog** | `map.setFog()` adds a smooth haze that blends the terrain into the sky. The `range` values are in *kilometres* of camera-space distance (the API internally converts them to metres). Adjust them until the horizon looks natural for your scene. |

---

## 3. Adding the sky to an *existing* style

If you already have a style JSON (e.g. a MapLibre style you fetched from a server) you can inject the sky layer **after** the map loads:

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // Insert the sky layer just before the first symbol layer (labels, icons, etc.)
  const layers = map.getStyle().layers;
  const firstSymbolId = layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'sky',
    type: 'sky',
    paint: {
      'sky-type': 'atmosphere',
      'sky-atmosphere-sun': [210, 30],
      'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 12
    }
  }, firstSymbolId);   // <-- insert before this layer (or omit to add on top)
});
```

Then enable terrain and fog exactly as shown above.

---

## 4. Fine-tuning the look

| Property | Typical values | What it does |
|----------|----------------|--------------|
| **`sky-atmosphere-sun`** | `[azimuth, altitude]` in degrees. Example: `[180, 45]` (south-west, 45° above horizon). | Controls where the sun appears. Move it to simulate different times of day. |
| **`sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity`** | `5 … 30` | Brightness of the sun. Lower → dusk/dawn, higher → midday. |
| **`sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend`** | `0 … 1` (default `0.2`) | How quickly the sky colour fades to the horizon colour. Larger = smoother transition. |
| **`sky-atmosphere-color`** | Any CSS colour, e.g. `"#88ccee"` | Overall tint of the sky (useful for sunrise/sunset). |
| **`map.setFog({color, range, "high-color"})`** | `color: "rgba(255,255,255,0.8)"`, `range: [0.5, 12]` | Controls the haze. Shorter `range` → thicker fog near the camera; longer → subtle atmospheric perspective. |
| **`map.setFog({ "space-color": "#000", "space-light-intensity": 0.2 })`** | (Only for space-type sky) | If you ever switch to a `sky-type: "gradient"` with a dark space colour, you can also control the “space” background. |

**Tip:** When you change the sun position you may also want to update the map’s `bearing` so the sun appears in the correct direction relative to the map orientation.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **No sky at all – just the page background** | Using a MapLibre version < 2.4, or the style does not contain a `sky` layer. | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre (`npm i maplibre-gl@latest` or use the CDN link above). Add a `sky` layer as shown. |
| **Sky appears but is completely black** | `sky-type` set to `"gradient"` with both colours set to `transparent` or missing. | Use `"sky-type": "atmosphere"` (or set explicit gradient colours). |
| **Fog looks like a solid white overlay** | `range` is too small (e.g. `[0, 1]`). | Increase the far value (`range: [0.5, 12]` or larger). |
| **Terrain disappears when I pitch > 80°** | `antialias: true` not set on map creation, or the canvas is being clipped by CSS. | Ensure `antialias: true` in the map options and that the map container has `overflow: hidden`. |
| **Sun moves but the sky colour does not change** | You are using `"sky-type": "gradient"` – the sun only affects `"atmosphere"` type. | Switch to `"sky-type": "atmosphere"` or add a custom gradient that you update manually. |

---

## 6. Going further – custom sky textures (advanced)

If you want a *non-realistic* sky (e.g. a painted sunset, starfield, or a video background) you can:

1. **Add a raster layer** that covers the whole world (e.g. a `type: "raster"` source with a sky image that is projected in EPSG:3857).  
2. **Place it at the very bottom** (`before: 'background'` or `before: 'terrain'`).  
3. **Set the map’s background to `transparent`** (`background-color: "rgba(0,0,0,0)"`) so the raster shows through.

However, for most use-cases the built-in `sky` layer + fog give a perfectly realistic result with far less effort.

---

## 7. TL;DR – Quick checklist

1. **Upgrade** to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.4.  
2. **Add a `sky` layer** (type `"sky"`, paint `"sky-type": "atmosphere"`).  
3. **Enable terrain** with `map.setTerrain({source: 'your-dem'})`.  
4. **Add fog** via `map.setFog({color, range})` for haze.  
