Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready recipe** for getting a proper sky (with a subtle haze/fog) on a MapLibre GL JS map that already has 3-D terrain and a steep pitch.

---

## 1. What you need

| Feature | How it’s provided |
|---------|-------------------|
| **Terrain** | `map.setTerrain({source: "terrain"})` (or the `terrain` property in the style) |
| **Sky** | A **`sky` layer** (type = `sky`). This draws the atmosphere, sun, and horizon. |
| **Haze / Fog** | The **`fog`** property on the style (or the `sky` layer’s `sky-horizon-blend`). |
| **Background colour** | Optional `background` layer (or `map.getCanvas().style.background`) – it only shows where the sky layer is not drawn (e.g., when you zoom out to a flat view). |

All three pieces are built-in to MapLibre GL JS v2.4 + (the version that ships with the `sky` layer). If you are on an older version, upgrade first.

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // ---- a plain background (will be hidden by the sky) ----
      "background": { "type": "background", "paint": { "background-color": "#e0e0ff" } },

      // ---- b. Terrain source (any DEM that MapLibre can read) ----
      "sources": {
        "terrain": {
          "type": "raster-dem",
          "url": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain/tiles.json", // demo DEM
          "tileSize": 512,
          "maxzoom": 14
        },
        // you also need a visual base-map (vector tiles, raster tiles, etc.)
        "osm": {
          "type": "raster",
          "tiles": ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          "tileSize": 256,
          "maxzoom": 19
        }
      },

      // ---- c. Layers (base map + sky) ----
      "layers": [
        // 1️⃣  Base map (just a simple raster for the demo)
        {
          "id": "osm",
          "type": "raster",
          "source": "osm"
        },

        // 2️⃣  Sky layer – this is the *real* sky
        {
          "id": "sky",
          "type": "sky",
          "paint": {
            // Choose the atmosphere model (gradient works too)
            "sky-type": "atmosphere",

            // Sun position – azimuth (0-360) and altitude (0-90) in degrees.
            // 0° azimuth = north, 90° = east.
            // Here we put the sun a little above the horizon in the south-west.
            "sky-atmosphere-sun": [210, 45],

            // Sun intensity – higher = brighter, more contrast.
            "sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity": 15,

            // How quickly the colour blends into the horizon.
            // 0 = hard line, 1 = very soft.
            "sky-horizon-blend": 0.4
          }
        }
      ],

      // -------------------------------------------------
      // 3️⃣  Fog – adds the “haze” that makes distant terrain fade.
      // -------------------------------------------------
      "fog": {
        // The colour of the fog (usually matches the sky colour)
        "color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.8)",

        // Where the fog starts (in metres) and where it becomes opaque.
        // Adjust these numbers to taste – smaller range = stronger haze.
        "range": [0.5, 12],

        // Optional: make the fog blend more with the horizon.
        "horizon-blend": 0.2
      }
    },

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Initial view – pitch 70°, a nice 3-D look
    // -------------------------------------------------
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco Bay Area
    zoom: 13,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: -30,
    antialias: true               // important for smooth terrain & sky
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Enable the terrain after the style loads
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣  (Optional) Add a simple UI to move the sun
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const sunControl = document.createElement('div');
  sunControl.style.position = 'absolute';
  sunControl.style.top = '10px';
  sunControl.style.right = '10px';
  sunControl.style.background = 'rgba(255,255,255,0.8)';
  sunControl.style.padding = '5px';
  sunControl.innerHTML = `
    <label>Sun azimuth <input id="az" type="range" min="0" max="360" value="210"></label><br>
    <label>Sun altitude <input id="alt" type="range" min="0" max="90" value="45"></label>
  `;
  document.body.appendChild(sunControl);

  sunControl.querySelector('#az').addEventListener('input', e => {
    const az = Number(e.target.value);
    const alt = Number(sunControl.querySelector('#alt').value);
    map.setPaintProperty('sky', 'sky-atmosphere-sun', [az, alt]);
  });
  sunControl.querySelector('#alt').addEventListener('input', e => {
    const alt = Number(e.target.value);
    const az = Number(sunControl.querySelector('#az').value);
    map.setPaintProperty('sky', 'sky-atmosphere-sun', [az, alt]);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Style → background** | Gives the page a neutral colour in case the sky layer fails to load. It’s completely covered by the sky when the map is pitched. |
| **Terrain source** | A raster-DEM (`raster-dem`). Replace the URL with your own DEM (e.g., a Mapbox Terrain-RGB tile set, a custom GeoTIFF-to-XYZ service, etc.). |
| **Base map layer** | Any visual layer you like (vector style, raster tiles, etc.). It’s rendered *under* the sky. |
| **`sky` layer** | The real sky. `sky-type: "atmosphere"` gives you a physically-based atmosphere with a sun, horizon glow, and a smooth colour gradient. |
| **`sky-atmosphere-sun`** | `[azimuth, altitude]` in degrees. Move these values to change where the sun appears. |
| **`sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity`** | Controls brightness/contrast of the sun and the overall sky. Typical values: 5-20. |
| **`sky-horizon-blend`** | How soft the transition is between sky colour and the ground. 0 = hard line, 1 = very soft. |
| **`fog`** | Adds distance-based haze. The `range` is in **kilometres** (MapLibre internally converts to metres). The lower the first number, the sooner distant terrain starts to fade. |
| **`antialias: true`** | Makes the terrain surface and sky edges look smooth, especially at high pitch. |
| **`map.setTerrain`** | Enables the DEM after the style is ready. You can also set `exaggeration` here to make mountains pop. |

---

## 2️⃣  Customising the look

### a) Gradient sky (no sun)

If you don’t need a sun, you can switch to a simple colour gradient:

```json
{
  "id": "sky",
  "type": "sky",
  "paint": {
    "sky-type": "gradient",
    "sky-gradient": [
      "interpolate",
      ["linear"],
      ["zoom"],
      0, "rgba(135,206,235,1)",   // light sky blue at far zoom-out
      12, "rgba(70,130,180,1)"   // deeper blue when you zoom in
    ],
    "sky-gradient-center": [0, 0],
    "sky-gradient-radius": 90,
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.3
  }
}
```

### b) Dark-mode / night sky

```json
{
  "id": "sky",
  "type": "sky",
  "paint": {
    "sky-type": "atmosphere",
    "sky-atmosphere-sun": [0, 0],          // sun below horizon → night
    "sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity": 0,
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "sky-atmosphere-color": "#0b1d2e",    // optional custom colour
    "sky-atmosphere-ground-color": "#0b1d2e"
  }
}
```

You can also combine a night sky with a **star field** by adding a raster layer that contains a star texture (or use a vector style that draws stars).

### c) Tweaking fog for “mountain haze”

```json
"fog": {
  "color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.85)",
  "range": [0.3, 8],          // start fading sooner, become opaque earlier
  "horizon-blend": 0.3
}
```

If you want a **brownish** mountain haze (common in desert scenes), change the colour:

```json
"fog": {
  "color": "rgba(210,180,140,0.7)",
  "range": [0.5, 10],
  "horizon-blend": 0.2
}
```

---

## 3️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Flat page colour shows above the horizon** | No `sky` layer, or the layer is placed *before* the base map layers (order matters). | Ensure the `sky` layer is **after** all ground layers (or at least after the terrain source). |
| **Sky looks like a solid colour, no gradient** | Using an old MapLibre version (< 2.4) that doesn’t support `sky`. | Upgrade to the latest `maplibre-gl` (≥ 2.4). |
| **Fog looks too strong / everything disappears** | `fog.range` is too small or the colour is too opaque. | Increase the second number in `range` (e.g., `[0.5, 20]`) and lower the alpha in `color`. |
| **Sun appears at the wrong spot** | `sky-atmosphere-sun` azimuth/altitude are swapped or out of range. | Azimuth: 0-360 (0 = north
